    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1017/s0033822200007414",
    "abstract": "The hottest topic for those interested in the earth's carbon cycles is the change in atmospheric CO 2 content between glacial and interglacial time. What caused it? What is its role in glacial cycles? We evaluate here the hypotheses that have been put forward to explain the CO 2 change with evidence from deep sea sediments. We conclude that all the hypotheses have serious drawbacks and that much effort will have to be expended in gathering more data from ice cores and ocean sediments before we will be pointed toward the correct scenario. Also, thoughtful modeling aimed at depicting the ties between pCO 2 , O 2 , 13 C/ 12 C, 14 C/ 12 C, and nutrient constituents in the sea for various modes of circulation will have to be done before the evidence from ocean cores can be properly interpreted.",
    "field": "Paleoclimatology",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the challenge of explaining the significant decrease in atmospheric CO\u2082 during glacial periods, as all existing hypotheses have serious drawbacks and struggle to reconcile conflicting evidence from ocean sediments and ice cores.",
    "method": "This paper evaluates existing hypotheses for the change in atmospheric CO\u2082 between glacial and interglacial periods and proposes that the correct scenario will be found by gathering more data from ice cores and ocean sediments and by developing more realistic models of the ocean's operation.",
    "task": "The task is to evaluate the various hypotheses proposed to explain the change in atmospheric CO\u2082 content between glacial and interglacial periods, using evidence from ice cores and deep sea sediments.",
